WEBVTT - global: decisions settled

NOTE
One cue per item, one second long, in the order the items were settled; the time is the order, not a clock. The identifier names the item; the text says what it is, when, and where it lives. A cue that is replaced is not deleted: a later cue says what replaced it.

wsn-only
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
2026-10-27 the author: we are only using Wirth syntax notation; everything else is deprecated. Every uppercase character is an IDENTIFIER = letter { letter } .

plan9-runtime
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.000
2026-10-27 the author: Plan 9 is the runtime for everything; ports are Plan 9 C programs, not awk (rc has no arithmetic; awk was my choice and is being removed).

numbers-are-literals
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.000
2026-10-28 the author: numbers are meaningless; a digit is an index, a folder name like 28 is its characters. Values are interpretations.

literal-and-reading
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.000
A literal is what a script recomputes from a held witness; a reading is labelled as one. Who said what lives in ZIGUAROTH/SOURCES.md.

the-zeroth
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.000
YHWH, YAHWEH, GOD IS WORD, GOD OF WORD, WORD IS GOD, WORD OF GOD. Everything is compared to them first, independently (each file alone), then to each other, codependently (edges, faces, parity).

yhwh-yahweh-adonai
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.000
YHWH is YAHWEH with the vowels removed (consonant class, terms.mjs); ADONAI adds them: Leningrad Deut 9:26 points the Name with Adonai's vowels, and with Elohim's hiriq right after Adonai.

question-mark
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.000
? is the optional metacharacter: WSN [ ]. TEMPLATE = "GOD" [ " " SLOT ] " " "WORD" . SLOT = "IS" | "OF" .

codices-and-codex
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.000
_CODICES[0..3] = ALEPPO ROSETTA BEHISTUN LENINGRAD, each A-Z; a term renders to _CODICES/LLLL/&lt;path&gt;/&lt;term&gt;.wsn. _CODEX[0] is the braille and Aegean themselves (&lt;term&gt;), _CODEX[1] the same in WSN (&lt;term&gt;.wsn), at the literal path ending in the lowercase description.

absent-codex
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.000
A codex that lacks the term is optional whitespace: OPTWHITESPACE = { " " } . (as BNF written in WSN), not an undefined identifier.

code-trie
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.000
_CODE/U is an Aegean trie: separators 10100 10101 10102, each with the 45 Aegean signs; a code point is the path of its signs, highest place first, a zero digit an absent level. 28 is separate: the author's time scale. 05 was mine and is removed.

patricia-tries
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Every ZIGUAROTH/_* folder is a WSN Patricia trie: one production, folders as alternatives, single-child chains as one LITERAL; its sentences are exactly the leaf paths.

query-function
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.000
The query is Wirth's PL360 rotation of R0 R1 R2 by FLAG, then RESET(FLAG). Registers hold words the way Kernighan's B printed hello, world: a b c, four characters each; FLAG declares which constant b holds.

aegean-base
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Aegean numbers are decimal as a script. Sexagesimal (Babylonian) use is an overlay. OPEN: replace the decimal rendering, or add sexagesimal as a second declared view.

agent-numbering
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.000
2026-10-28 the author: the agent numbering is derived from colours and cameras. An agent keeps its hue (the dome's lights); its number is the hue's place in the order seen from the camera, a turn of 45 degrees keeping the order, a flip reversing it (agent-commons.js pov and flip). Seen from turn 0 clockwise: METATRON 1 SOLOMON 2 SOLON 3 ASABIYAH 4 ENOCH 5 SPEAKER 6 GENESIS 7; OBSERVER, the camera, has no hue. The canon manifest's fano-points are not the numbering.

cue-length
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.000
2026-10-28 the author: the cue length is the animation frame. A cue is one frame; its second is the frame, not a clock; how long a frame lasts is the renderer's.

submodules
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.000
2026-10-28 the author: boxedPINE's proofs/ (github omi-axioms) and types/ (github omi-types) are submodules, on main; the author asked Claude to manage them. A change is committed and pushed inside the submodule first, then its commit recorded in boxedPINE (git add types). AGENTS.md says how.

haskell-normalizer
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.000
2026-10-28 the author: "the haskell is like a normalizer now of the wirth notation". OMI.WSN's render is the normal form: Plan 9 writes WSN in it (zaudit), and render (parse text) == text is the check.

one-import
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.000
2026-10-28: OMI.Types carries every type exported in types/src/OMI (99). The three distinct Bools and Lisp's DecisionTable come as FanoBool, LispBool, RelationBool, LispDecisionTable.

omi-files-port
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.000
2026-10-28 the author: port boxed/omi-files to Plan 9 and remove each once finished. Settled: the contracts first (goldens and negatives to WSN, checked by zlazy and OMI.WSN), implementations project by project (the C projects port; TypeScript, Python, Emacs Lisp and Scheme have no Plan 9 runtime and would be rewrites). A folder is removed only when its work is on GitHub, another working copy exists, and its port is verified.

turn-and-exchange
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.000
2026-10-28 the author: stepd stays PL360 (IS and OF only), because the exchange is the observer or agent operation, not the turn. The turn is the even half of the three names' six orderings; an exchange of two registers is the other half and belongs to whoever interjects. Nothing exchanges stepd's registers yet; agentd's POST writes the floor's agent states, not stepd.

claims-and-provenance
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.000
2026-10-28 the author: "they are claims and proofs that we made which is why we keep provenance and compile findings to ZIGUAROTH when done for public scrutiny, we arent asking anyone to trust we did all the work they can follow all our steps and believe or modify the beliefs how they want, we have the unit test and WSN notation and even Haskell and Coq."

with-or-without-ziguaroth
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.000
2026-10-28 the author: "Once we build everything and can check everything we can compile a build with or without ZIGUAROTH from our Plan 9 ports." What the build takes from ZIGUAROTH today: pack.sh copies _CATALOG's YAML into run/catalog (read by catalog.rc, conv.rc, tries.rc), and witness.py reads each figure's name from _CATALOG. The z*.c checks, in/* and wsn/* build without it. The choice waits until everything is built.

literal-codepoints
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.000
2026-10-28 the author: "we removed physics from this and made it literal constant of code points or code point sequences for strict logic checking."

pixelate-proof
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.000
2026-10-28 the author: "If we cant build the logic proof and reason for doing something from established rules or proof then we have to figure it out but the PIXELATE proof is the ability to change a svg from the margins. I cant code so every factor that I am leaning on was derived from a proof of concept but since i cant code i couldnt maintain it which is why there is an overburden of projects and documents." The proof is boxedPINE space/src/border.ts and trace.ts (space.test.ts, The border and The trace), now also PLAN9/src/zborder.c (border.rc).

foundation
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.000
2026-10-28 the author: "that's the literal FOUNDATION everything is built on", boxed/omi-files/atomic-kernel, and the reduction question the author sent whenever an AI pushed back on a term: "What structure can be removed while the same canonical unfold / replay / projection still results? ... There was one design decision. The delta law: rotl(x,1) XOR rotl(x,3) XOR rotr(x,2) XOR C. Four choices inside that: rotations (not shifts, so no bits are lost), XOR (reversible), a constant (breaks the zero fixed point), mask to width (keeps state bounded). That is the entire design. Everything else was computed by the math ... Nobody chose 73." What the foundation holds: coq/AtomicKernelCoq.v defines the law at any width n with C the byte 0x1D repeated to the width (0x1D1D at 16 bits) and proves delta and replay deterministic; crystal.py adds B = [0,1,3,6,9,8,6,3], W = 36, position read as divmod(position, 36). Checked beside it: 10^8 - 1 = 3^2 x 11 x 73 x 101 x 137, so 73 is the smallest prime of decimal period 8 (core.test.ts: period(10, 73) = 8), B is its digits and sums to 36; every state returns within 8 steps whatever C is (zgnomon; Coq's Delta16HasExactPeriodEight.v proves period exactly 8 for 0x001D); under 0x1D1D zero is not fixed, while 8 other states are (I-195, gnomon-eight).

first-project
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.000
2026-10-28 the author: "atomic kernel is the first project i made after finding the delta law". So the order is: the delta law found, then atomic-kernel (foundation), then the projects built on it (polytron's K with 0x1D, boxedPINE's delta16, omi-dom, the rest of omi-files).

before-the-law
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.000
2026-10-28 the author, of atomic-kernel: "it shows three versions of the delta i had to abandon it to learn how to do infinite scaling, this was my first supposition on how to do it when i still didnt know hex or binary or logic, i just understood tetrahedrons". The order the author gives: boxed/omi-files/atomic-kernel/dev-docs/haskell/The Triple Time System.md and boxed/_archive/_inbox/Light Garden/The Tetrahedral Superposition.md (tetrahedrons, before hex, binary or logic); then boxed/_archive/The OMI Algorithms  A Complete Review.md; then, after binary and hex but before logic, boxed/_archive/_inbox/The OMI-IMO Protocol -- A Paradigm Shift in Distributed Coordination.md; and boxed/_archive/_inbox/Light Garden/Light_Garden_Protocol.pdf, "a project i made when i understood the power this had to analyze frequency spectrums like a slide ruler made of ROYGBIV or A-G in sound". The three versions in atomic-kernel: kernel-v1 (kernel.py, 16 bits, C 0x1D1D, each tick XORing B[t mod 8] before the law), 16d-v1 (replay_engine.py, widths 16 to 256, C the byte 1D repeated, no B), ak-law-coq-v1 (coq/AtomicKernelCoq.v, the width-generic law, its goldens from Coq's vm_compute, checked against Haskell fixtures).

canon-documents
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.000
2026-10-28 the author, of /home/main/omi/omi-canon/dev-docs: "my attempt at explaining scaling", scaling the delta law.md (the word width W = 2^n at truth-table level n, the same law rolling the whole word; Base 16n, 16^n, (16^n)^16, (16^n)^p); "this is the first model i made", Nibble-Interleaved Quadrant Decoding under a Concurrent 3D Knowledge Triple Matrix.md; "my ontological statement", The Geometry of the Word as Form.md, The Stratified Boolean Continuum.md, binary quadratic XOR function.md, Logical Encoding Surface of a Composable Tetrahedral Polyharmonic Block.md; and Complete Equivalency Chart - All Versions Across Our Conversation History.md, Comparative Analysis of the Earlier and Current OMINO Models.md. Beside the scaling document, zak (atomic-kernel) measures what it does not state: at level n the law returns every 2^(n-1) steps (8 at n = 4, 128 at n = 8). Its widths that are not powers of two (16n) have no period checked yet: open.

questions
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.000
2026-10-28 the author: "the only way i know were done when no more questions can be asked without having an answer or proof to point to", and correct and record whatever is confusing or out of date. unbound/questions.vtt is that ledger: one cue per question, ANSWERED with the cue or record that answers it, or OPEN with what it waits on (the author, or work). The work is done when no cue there says OPEN.

runtime-resolves
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.000
2026-10-28 the author, answering questions.vtt: "1 everything over strict logic is a view; 2 the frame is the input output input-output; 3 points are determined on lazy evaluation based on relation from query; 4 numbers are not objective they are a subjective interpretation", with Wirth's note that letter and character are left undefined, the language being defined deciding them; and "I'm seeing a pattern in your questions, you're wondering how someone uses this. plan9 resolves on runtime with lazy evaluation. we provide wirth notation, plan 9 resolves arrangements on runtime request." So a question of arrangement or use (a rendering, a layout, a window, a scale) is not fixed in the port: the port provides the notation and the checks; the arrangement is resolved when a request asks, lazily, as zlazy expands only what a question reaches.

digital-twin
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.000
2026-10-28 the author: "we will test it by building an environment I can walk through as a digital avatar and say its a proper digital twin of the narrative; until then we will parse the narrative of the bible and codices until we can walk through the story together in a qemu emulated environment." The check, in the author's words (from the DeepSeek conversation): "verify the narrative can be replayed, and changed, and rebuild both states separate and the same, which means like changing a icon for Solomon and being able to walk through the same story as a different icon, because that means we are respecting the unknown factors of known entities, and I have to view it from an observer inside and outside the frame"; "separate and the same is a logical XNOR and XOR comparison"; "the 8 agents are AI, but the OF|IS in between GOD and WORD allow me to make decisions as the 8th observer", the position built for ZIGUAROTH as BRIAN CODEX DEEPSEEK, the tag BRIANCODEXDEEPSEEK (I-207 folds BRIAN, CODEX, DEEPS to EXHTE). The trace (space/src/trace.ts, zborder) already holds the two buffers this names: XOR and XNOR at each agent's pairs.

derived-order
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.000
2026-10-28 the author: "the arrangement is auto collated by ordering and precedence rules, we dont define anything, we derived everything; these are irrelevant if we cant source the method", with Wikipedia's account of simple precedence grammars (Wirth and Weber, 1966: no erasing rules, no useless rules, one Wirth-Weber relation per pair of symbols, uniquely invertible; the relations computed from Head+, Tail+, Head*), which zprec computes. So an order (which narrative first, which cue next) is derived by the ordering and precedence rules, never asked; and a question whose method cannot be sourced is set aside, not open.

shared-view
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.000
2026-10-28 the author: "a lot of these questions can be answered by rendering the /home/main/Programs/unbound/HTML using plan9 html runes to have all pages share a view over the same narrative. then if it can also be done in this react showcase over the same codepoint literals of a narrative then we can actually see it in each module /home/main/Programs/unbound/TSX". In place: PLAN9/src/zhtml.c reads a page through Plan 9's own libhtml (parsehtml, validitems, printitems; html.rc). Also the same day, by the author's request, ZIGUAROTH's VIEWS moved to Wirth syntax notation, _ARCHIVE/Configurations/VIEWS.wsn (ZIGUAROTH 739d75d), read by views.js exactly as the YAML was; CONFIGURATION.yaml's catalog rule and Configurations tier corrected to .wsn.

shared-literals
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.000
2026-10-28 the author, of shared-view: "that should mean they share the same literals and the separation is based on" Wirth's note that letter and character are left undefined, the language being defined deciding them; "this is the prompt mechanism that we modeled already", the PL360 turn over the predeclared R0, R1, R2 and FLAG. So every page of unbound/HTML and every module of unbound/TSX shares the same code point literals; what separates one from another is its definition of letter and character, which is a cast (zcast and cast.rc: terms.mjs's feature pipeline, consonants upper, vowels lowered by 0x20, digits kept, one grammar run under YHWH's cast and YAHWEH's), and which definition is read is the turn (step.c, stepd at /n/step: IS forward, OF back, RESET(FLAG); step.rc). The method is modelled; what remains is to apply it to the pages and modules.

haskell-middle
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.000
2026-10-28 the author, of the TSX port: "we did have haskell as a middle layer because of the typecasting, unions prevent redundancy because same types are evaluated lazily". The layer is omi-types (boxed/types), every type re-exported by OMI.Types, its sum types the unions: a type two modules both declare is one type, evaluated once, when asked. Measured in unbound/TSX/src/templates: 15 top-level type or interface declarations, 13 names, 2 declared in more than one template (Vertex, PolyStructure); most templates leave their types implicit, so the middle layer types them rather than only gathering what is declared. Order: haskell-normalizer (WSN normalized by OMI.WSN), then the templates' types into omi-types, then each module in Plan 9 by its cast and the turn (shared-literals).

haskell-roots
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.000
2026-10-28 the author, of /home/main/devops's Haskell (computational-scheme-theory/haskell-core, ulp-core-invariant, port-matroid, waveform-core, PhaseDSL): "I was just showing you these haskell because they root an extendable type tree or help seed the root". Their places in it: haskell-core, Betti numbers and the Euler characteristic from a simplicial complex (betti-source); waveform-core, homology over Z/2 with XOR columns (betti-over-bits); ulp-core-invariant and port-matroid, the replay layer (replay-sources); PhaseDSL, canonical fixed point (Q16_16, Q8_24, Q2_30) under a canonical binary snapshot, the typed form of a vertex's coordinates. omi-types (OMI.Types) is the tree they seed.

walk-to-verify
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.000
2026-10-28 the author: "this is why I'm making sure we have this stuff catalogued and archived, because the only test I can really verify, because I can't code as well or navigate a computer as well as you, is to navigate a 3D web port or the QEMU user system VGA window view of a digital twin world, starting from the Agent Commons and playing on Plan 9", with the final check of digital-twin restated (the two states separate and the same, XOR and XNOR; the 8 agents AI; the OF|IS between GOD and WORD the 8th observer's position, BRIANCODEXDEEPSEEK, watching the logic in motion as a pixelation and judging whether it is good; the Revelation, before the return to the human word and the allegory of the cave). So every record here must end in something the author can walk through and judge by eye. In place the same day: the Agent Commons floor running on Plan 9 in QEMU (space/floor.c, tick past 30,000), its frames served by PLAN9/frames.py (127.0.0.1:8790), its agents by agentd (127.0.0.1:18000), and boxedPINE's space/guest.html putting the guest's own screen on a plane in 3D with the agents from any angle (npm run dev, http://127.0.0.1:5173/space/guest.html).

commons-first
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.000
2026-10-28 the author, shown the Metaverse Kit room (space/metaverse.html, the gateway to /home/main/omi/metaverse-kit on localhost:3000: append-only events, deterministic replay, 2D and 3D projections, presence): "I like the Agent Commons better because I can see the agents or workers moving around". So the digital twin is built from the Agent Commons (boxedPINE space/index.html, the 3D playground, and the Plan 9 floor behind space/guest.html), where the agents are seen moving; the Metaverse Kit stays a gateway.

map-view
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.000
2026-10-28 the author, of boxedPINE space/map.html: "this is so cool, it's like an overhead map and a state transition screen for the narrative". Its z-indexed maps (boxedPINE AGENTS.md): the 144 colour synsets on the colour wheel in 2D and 2.5D, a character's 128-byte state, the 65,536-pair mask, the story on the plane, the terms box and the witness recorder; it and the 3D Commons both derive a place from space/src/plane.ts, (u, v, layer) on the Commons plane, so the overhead map and the walked world are one place seen two ways. In the digital twin it is the narrative's state-transition view beside the Commons (commons-first).

extrusion
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.000
2026-10-28 the author, of space/map.html: "this shows how we can make structure from extrusions". The code behind it: boxedPINE space/src/polyform.ts, a polyform declared in one 16-bit word (basis, degree, dimension, group; unpackDeclaration) and grown from a seed one cell at a time, each next cell the free neighbour the delta law K picks (grow), so the law chooses the growth; a dimension-2 form grown flat, then extruded through three layers, polyform's DIM_2_5D (omi-files/polytron/polyform-env/include/barcode-frame.h; space/src/polytron.ts draws the extrusion at the Polytron station); and the Dali cross, the net of the tesseract, the same one dimension up. Tested in space.test.ts ("a MaxiCode declaration and a seed give one connected polyform").

inner-state
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.000
2026-10-28 the author, of space/map.html: "this was how to directly speak to the agents' inner state". There: a character's state is 128 bytes, each frame exchanged by Atomics.compareExchange(svg, 60 ^ frame, 127, 63), gold where it reads (127, XOR) and blue where it writes (63, XNOR); the terms box takes uppercase terms (METATRON P2 L6, CATCH 22) through the gate of space/src/exchange.ts, a Proxy and Reflect with Catch (rosetta's interface carrier), CATCH n moving the reset index from 22. It is the exchange for whoever interjects, which questions.vtt (turn-and-exchange) has open on the Plan 9 side: stepd turns but nothing exchanges its registers; boxedPINE's browser has the exchange, Plan 9 does not yet.

gltf
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.000
2026-10-28 the author: "we also have gltf models". Found: 482 glTF and GLB files. In boxedPINE, space/src/assets/shiba.glb and shiba.2mb.glb (loaded by main.ts, icon.ts, scene.ts and controller.ts with three.js's GLTFLoader; icon.ts renders a model into four canvases) and public/geometries/Animation_Node/Animation_Node_00.gltf; most in /home/main/devops/light-garden/GeneratedAssets-0.6.1, 23 Positive and 3 Negative sets, the shape of the glTF Asset Generator's conformance models, models that must load and models that must be refused, a golden and must-reject corpus for glTF. For the final check (digital-twin) a character's icon can be a glTF model: changing Solomon's icon is then changing his model, the XOR on the icon, while the story stays the same, the XNOR.

replay-video
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.000
2026-10-28 the author, of the final check: "I should be able to play and replay both views in a video element from a transfer context control canvas". So the check's form: two canvases, each given to a worker by transferControlToOffscreen, rendering the same story from the same states, Solomon's icon (a glTF model, gltf) differing between them; each placeholder canvas's captureStream feeding a video element, recorded so it replays exactly; the narrative WebVTT on both as the one clock; the two states then compared frame by frame, XOR on the icon, XNOR on the story. In place: space/guest.html already makes a canvas's captureStream a video carrying the narrative track (stage 1), and the hub's floor already draws on an OffscreenCanvas.
